  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM EST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nSurface high pressure just east of the Ohio River Valley and mid \nlevel ridging passing from the Plains States to the Mid Atlantic \nStates is in control of the short term's weather providing dry \nconditions through late Saturday night. With high pressure to \nour east, modest WAA allows high temps to slowly warm between \ntoday and Friday so that all areas are securely in the 60s. Lows\nstay above freezing and this will continue to be the case into \nthe first half next week. We'll also have some breezy winds \nThursday around 20 to 25 mph.\n\nTemperatures drop Sunday as the moisture column becomes more \nsaturated. A theta-e plume with connection to the Gulf swings \nthrough the area with PWAT standardized anomalies over 2. Even \nstill, the arrival of the front will need to be watched because with \nits early arrival in the midday to early afternoon, maximum heating \nand instability formation is likely to be stunted limiting \nthunderstorm potential. Therefore, precipitation is expected to \ntotal 0.1\" to 0.25\" across the area with the highest residing in the \nsoutheast portion of the area. Behind this cold front, high temps \nwill drop through the 50s and level out in the 40s the next work \nweek with below freezing lows back in the picture for the end of \nnext week. This is as 850 mb temperatures fall below 0C and the -10C \ncontour gets near the area late Monday. Instability is generated on \nMonday as an area of cold air moves over the warmer lakes/ground, \nbut the lake induced instability is fairly modest and the moisture \ncolumn is rather dry. Low level lapse rates approach dry adiabatic \nthough.",
